TLlama for Debian
=================

The tllama package installs both /usr/bin/tllama (client) and /usr/bin/tllamad
(foreground daemon), using the system Python. It depends on tllama-server,
whose metapackage supplies the private native runtime and CPU backend. Install
additional tllama-server backend packages for GPU acceleration. Models are
not included and are not downloaded by package installation.

Configuration and service
-------------------------

/etc/tllama/server.toml is a dpkg-managed conffile. Local edits are retained
on upgrade through the normal dpkg configuration-file mechanism. The unit
reads this file explicitly; CLI > environment > TOML > built-in defaults.
Use systemctl edit tllamad.service for unit overrides.

Client configuration is separate from the daemon configuration. Examples are
installed in /usr/share/doc/tllama/examples/client.toml and server.toml.
The client example is not enabled automatically. For system defaults, copy it
to /etc/tllama/client.toml; for user preferences, copy it to
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tllama/client.toml (normally ~/.config/tllama/client.toml).
Built-in defaults < system file < user file; explicit --config reads only the
selected file over built-in defaults. Supported environment values and CLI
flags override file values. See docs/cli.md for the full precedence rules.
The upstream CHANGELOG.md includes release changes and upgrade notes.

The service is enabled and started on installation, subject to the host's
service policy. It listens on 127.0.0.1:54800 by default, without preloading
a model. Upgrades stop the old daemon and its workers before replacing code,
then start the service through debhelper's standard service-policy handling.
Removal stops the service; purge clears its packaged registration. A local
systemd mask remains effective.

The package creates a static system account and group named tllama. If the
render and/or video device groups exist, the account is added to them on
configuration. Backend driver permissions may require further local setup.
The package does not create device groups or change device-node permissions.

Systemd manages these directories:

  /var/lib/tllama       persistent state, including models/ (mode 0750)
  /var/cache/tllama     cache, including huggingface/ (mode 0750)
  /run/tllama           private worker IPC (mode 0700)

They are owned by tllama. Custom model paths require appropriate access for
that account. Configuration paths are relative to the service working
directory, /var/lib/tllama. Purge removes packaged configuration and service
registration, but deliberately retains downloaded models, state, caches and
the service account. Remove retained data manually only when no longer needed.

If upgrading from a manual unit installed at
/etc/systemd/system/tllamad.service, that local unit takes precedence over the
packaged unit in /usr/lib/systemd/system. Review and remove the manual copy
when adopting the packaged unit, then run systemctl daemon-reload. Preserve
intentional customizations as a systemd drop-in.

Build and versioning
--------------------

Build on Debian 13 with the required dependencies available in the configured
APT repositories. Some upstream dependency floors need local backports;
notably AnyIO >= 4.9 and GGUF >= 0.17.1. The build does not use pip or download
Python dependencies. A missing or too-old dependency must be packaged first.
Hugging Face support is installed as a runtime dependency so pull works.

  sudo apt-get build-dep .
  debuild -us -uc -b

For a source build, supply the matching tllama_<upstream>.orig.tar.gz alongside
the checkout, then use debuild -us -uc. After creating the v1.1.0 release tag:

  git archive --format=tar --prefix=tllama-1.1.0/ v1.1.0 |
    gzip -n > ../tllama_1.1.0.orig.tar.gz
  debuild -us -uc

The package uses source format 3.0 (quilt). debian/changelog owns the Debian
version. Debian's epoch and revision are excluded from the Python metadata;
the prerelease tilde is removed for PEP 440. The rules export
SETUPTOOLS_SCM_PRETEND_VERSION_FOR_TLLAMA, so a Git-less source build produces
the same version: 1.0.0~rc1-1 reports 1.0.0rc1, and 1.0.0-1 reports 1.0.0.
This does not create or move a Git release tag. Bump the changelog when the
next release is prepared.

The build runs a small offline application smoke against the built package;
DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck / DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=nocheck can skip it in the usual
Debian way. The full development pytest suite is separate. The same smoke is
registered as an autopkgtest and checks installed metadata, entry points,
configuration validation and the in-process /api/version endpoint. It loads
no model, starts no listener and requires no GPU.
